    "summary": "High‑energy Sacramento set with soaring Playin’, monster Other One > Truckin’ jam",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blistering Playing In The Band with wah‑pedal leads, a fiery Bird Song, and an epic Other One → Black Peter sandwich that launches into a high‑gear Truckin’. Keith’s jazzy keyboards and Phil’s explosive bass anchor the jams, while Donna’s vocal on Across the Rio Grande divides opinion. Soundboard recordings capture the performance clearly, making it a must‑listen for ’72 fans.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Sacramento Memorial Auditorium on August 12, 1972 finds the Dead riding a wave of youthful exuberance that defines their early‑'70s summer stretch. The first set builds momentum with tight renditions of Bird Song and a ripping Playing In The Band where Garcia’s wah‑pedal screams over a thunderous rhythm section; Donna Jean’s vocal cameo on Across the Rio Grande adds a quirky flavor even if it splits the crowd. The second set is the real highlight: after an emphatic He's Gone, Bill drives into a seamless Drums → Other One → Black Peter sandwich that erupts into a soaring Truckin', followed by a crisp Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo and a bright Sugar Magnolia finale. Keith Godchaux’s piano and Rhodes weave jazzy textures while Phil Lesh drops “bombs” throughout, especially the iconic bass intro to The Other One. Across the board the band is locked in, making this Sacramento show one of the most compelling snapshots of the 1972 tour.",
